-- title:
--   Six addition-law coordinates generate the unit ideal on each chart pair
-- statement:
--   Let $R$ be a commutative ring, and let $W$ be a Weierstrass curve over $R$ which is elliptic in the sense of Mathlib's `IsElliptic` class (its discriminant is a unit), and assume moreover that $2$ is invertible in $R$. The conclusion is the proposition `KwLRSixUCoverage W`, which unfolds to: for every pair of indices $i, j \in \{0,1,2\}$ (`Fin 3`), the ideal of the tensor product ring $(\mathcal{A}\,i) \otimes_R (\mathcal{A}\,j)$ generated by the image of the family `kw_lrSixU W i j` is the whole ring $\top$. That family is indexed by `Fin 3 ⊕ Fin 3` and is given by `Sum.elim` of the two triples `kw_lrChart_u W i j` and `kw_lrSymChart_u W i j`; so the assertion is that the six elements consisting of the three coordinates of the chord addition law and the three coordinates of the symmetric addition law, dehomogenised to the chart-pair tensor algebra indexed by $(i,j)$, generate the unit ideal there.
--
--   This is the covering statement underlying the Lange–Ruppert/Bosma–Lenstra style construction of the group law on a projective Weierstrass model: on each of the nine chart pairs of the self-product, the chord and symmetric addition laws have no common zero, so their loci of definition cover the product. It is used as one conjunct of the gluing data for [`WeierstrassProjModel.addMorphism_gluing`](thm.html#WeierstrassProjModel.addMorphism_gluing), which assembles the two laws into a single addition morphism.
